Title: Flipped
Author: Nader Nassereddine
Draft date: May 2026 — Revision 2
Genre: Psychological Thriller
Format: Short Film / Pilot (~38 pages)
Contact:

===

FADE IN:

                                                      BLACK.

A sound: applause. Distant, growing.

Then a voice — warm, measured, the voice of a man who has
learned to make silence feel like a gift he is giving you.

McGEE (V.O.)
I don't think of myself as a therapist.

The applause swells.

McGEE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Therapists fix things. I'm more
interested in what happens when you
stop trying to fix yourself.

FADE UP SLOWLY ON:

INT. McGEE'S HOME STUDY - DAY (INTERVIEW)

The room is extraordinary. Not ostentatious — curated.
Every object chosen to say: *I have taste, and taste is
a form of intelligence.* Books, real ones, not arranged
for the camera. Morning light. A ficus that costs more
than most people's therapy.

DR. BRIAN McGEE (52) sits in a low leather chair. He is
not performing relaxation. He simply IS relaxed, which
is more frightening.

Across from him, barely visible: a JOURNALIST, late 30s,
recorder on the arm of her chair.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
How do you respond to critics who
say your methods aren't clinically
validated?

McGEE
(a small smile)
I respond with forty-three million
podcast listeners.

He adjusts his cuff. Not a power move. Just a habit.

McGEE (CONT'D)
Clinical validation is a language
invented by institutions to protect
themselves from disruption. I don't
speak that language. My patients do,
though. They speak it in the past tense.

SMASH CUT TO:

INT. ARENA - BACKSTAGE CORRIDOR - NIGHT

Fluorescent. Industrial. The ugly infrastructure under the
beautiful surface.

McGee walks the corridor flanked by DEREK (28, his assistant,
moving like a man who has learned to anticipate) and a
SECURITY DETAIL. He's in the same open-collar shirt he'll
wear on stage — chosen, like everything, to look unchosen.

The roar of the crowd leaks through the walls.

McGee doesn't look at anyone. He's somewhere else — running
something internal, like an athlete before a race.

DEREK
Twelve thousand seats. Full.

McGEE
(not breaking stride)
Overflow?

DEREK
Two auxiliary rooms. Screens.
Another four thousand.

McGee nods once. Sixteen thousand people.

DEREK (CONT'D)
The Chicago Tribune piece drops at
ten. The pre-embargo reviews are —

McGEE
I know what the reviews say.

He stops at a mirror mounted on a cinder block wall.
Looks at himself. Adjusts nothing.

McGEE (CONT'D)
What's the dependency metric on
the intensive cohort this quarter?

DEREK
(checking his phone)
Eighty-one percent retention past
the six-month mark. Up from seventy-
four last quarter.

McGEE
And the volunteer conversion rate?

DEREK
Sixty-three percent of intensive
graduates are now in the Ambassador
program.

McGee looks at himself in the mirror a moment longer.

McGEE
Good.

He walks away. The mirror holds his reflection a beat
after he's gone.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S HOME STUDY - DAY (INTERVIEW - CONTINUOUS)

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Your new book is called
*The Unbroken Self.* The thesis, as
I understand it, is that the concept
of psychological damage is itself
a form of damage.

McGEE
That's close. The fuller version is:
we have built an entire industry around
the word *healing* — and healing implies
that something is wrong with you.

He leans forward, just slightly.

McGEE (CONT'D)
Nothing is wrong with you. You are
having a human experience. The pain
is the experience. And what I offer
people —

(a pause, choosing the word)

— is company. In the pain. Not rescue.

The journalist writes something.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
And the two hundred dollars an hour
for that company?

McGEE
(without missing a beat)
Is a bargain, compared to a lifetime
of pretending you're fine.

He smiles. It is genuinely warm.

Then:

                                        GLITCH.

The image stutters — a frame skip, an audio dropout.
Half a second of visual noise.

McGEE
(same tone, same smile)
Is a bargain, compared to the cost
of them leaving.

                                        BACK.

McGee finishes the smile. Reaches for his coffee.

CUT TO:

INT. ARENA - BACKSTAGE - MOMENTS BEFORE STAGE - NIGHT

Derek hands McGee a wireless mic. McGee clips it on without
looking, the way you fasten a seatbelt.

The crowd noise is enormous now. Sixteen thousand people
who drove here, flew here, took time off work and paid
two hundred dollars for a seat — to be in the same room
as this man.

A PRODUCTION ASSISTANT holds up three fingers. Two. One.

McGEE
(quietly, to no one)
Here we go.

He walks into the light.

CUT TO:

INT. ARENA - STAGE - CONTINUOUS - NIGHT

The stage is minimal. One spotlight, one man. Behind him,
projected twenty feet high: the cover of *The Unbroken Self.*

The crowd gives him a standing ovation before he speaks
a word. This is what he has built.

McGEE
(into the mic, after a long moment)
Sit down. Please.

(they don't)

No, really. Sit down. I'm not here
to be applauded. I'm here to work.

They sit. He has their complete attention.

McGEE (CONT'D)
I want to start tonight with a question.

He begins to move.

McGEE (CONT'D)
When is the last time someone sat
across from you — really sat across
from you — and made you feel like the
most important thing in the room?

Silence.

McGEE (CONT'D)
Not because they loved you. Not because
they wanted something. But because in
that moment, you were genuinely the
most interesting thing they had ever
encountered.

He stops. Looks directly at the audience.

McGEE (CONT'D)
That is what I do.

The audience erupts again.

He lets them.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S HOME STUDY - DAY (INTERVIEW)

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Tell me about the people who come
to you. Who is the typical patient?

McGEE
There's no typical patient. There's
a typical wound.

He picks up his coffee cup. Turns it slowly.

McGEE (CONT'D)
Loss. The feeling of being
fundamentally misunderstood. The
gap between who someone believes
they are and who the world seems
to think they are.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
And you close that gap?

McGEE
I make the gap liveable.

(beat)

Which, to most people, feels like
closing it.

He looks at the window. Something passes across his face —
not regret. More like noting a fact.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Is that the same thing?

McGEE
(back to her)
Ask me again in five years.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Dr. McGee —

She hesitates. He waits. He is very good at waiting.

JOURNALIST (O.S.) (CONT'D)
Do you actually help people?

A long beat. He considers the question as if it is the
most interesting thing he's been asked in years.

                                        GLITCH.

McGEE
No.

                                        BACK.

McGEE (CONT'D)
Help is a loaded word. I give
people a framework. What they do
with it is their own.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
And the ones who don't do well
within the framework?

McGEE
(gently)
No framework works for everyone.
That's not a failure of the system.

He sets down his cup.

McGEE (CONT'D)
That's data.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S OFFICE - DAY

SESSION: PATIENT ONE

A different kind of quiet than Nadia's sessions. Softer.

THOMAS (44, construction foreman, hands that have built
things) sits with his arms on his knees, looking at the
floor. He has been crying recently — not now, but recently.

McGee sits across from him. Unhurried. Present.

THOMAS
I keep thinking about the accident.
Like — not the whole thing. Just one
detail. The sound the car made.

McGEE
Tell me about that sound.

THOMAS
It's like — I can't describe it.
Metal and something else. Something
I didn't want to know was in there.

A long pause.

THOMAS (CONT'D)
I was driving.

McGEE
Yes.

THOMAS
I wasn't — it wasn't my fault.
The police said —

McGEE
I know what the police said.

He leans in.

McGEE (CONT'D)
What do you say?

Thomas looks up at him. This is what McGee does — this
specific move, this specific gentleness — that makes
people feel found.

THOMAS
I should have seen him.

McGEE
(quietly)
Yes.

Not agreement. Not absolution. Just acknowledgment.
And somehow that is more powerful than either.

Thomas exhales.

McGEE (CONT'D)
Let's sit with that.

They do.

What the audience sees: a gifted therapist meeting a
man in his pain.

What we will later understand: McGee has identified the
exact word — *should* — and filed it. He will use it
again. He always does.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S HOME STUDY - DAY (INTERVIEW)

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
How many patients do you see
in a typical week?

McGEE
I have twelve private patients.
The intensive program serves about
three hundred at any given time,
through my certified practitioners.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
And the podcast reaches —

McGEE
Forty-three million, as of last month.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
That's a lot of people to be
responsible for.

McGEE
(the word lands wrong, and he notices)
I'm not responsible for them.

A fractional pause.

McGEE (CONT'D)
I'm responsible *to* them. There's
a meaningful difference.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
What's the difference?

McGEE
Responsibility *for* someone
implies they can't manage themselves.
Responsibility *to* someone says:
I show up. I do my part.

He opens his hands.

McGEE (CONT'D)
What they do with it is theirs.

                                        GLITCH.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Do you ever feel guilty?

McGEE
(without hesitation)
No.

                                        BACK.

McGEE (CONT'D)
Guilt is a signal. I take it
seriously when it comes. It doesn't
come often in this work. I believe
in what I do.

He means it. Both things are true: he never feels guilty.
And he believes in what he does. The horror is that these
facts coexist without friction.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S OFFICE - DAY

SESSION: PATIENT TWO

RENATA (31, graduate student, very thin in the way people
become when anxiety is the primary caloric expense) sits
on the couch with her shoes off. Comfortable here. Too
comfortable — the comfort of a place you have been too
many times.

This is Session Thirty-One. We know this because Renata
mentions it.

RENATA
Brian, I've been thinking about
what you said last week. About the
"protective architecture."

McGEE
And?

RENATA
I think I've been doing it my
whole life. Building walls so nobody
could —

She stops. Puts her hand over her mouth.

RENATA (CONT'D)
God. I can see it so clearly now.
How nobody was ever actually allowed
in. Even my mom. Even —

McGEE
Even Marcus?

She flinches. Marcus was her fiancé. He left eight months
ago, two months after she started seeing McGee.

RENATA
He couldn't handle the process.
You said that. You said some people
in our lives aren't equipped to
be with us when we're —

McGEE
I said that people who are invested
in our *stasis* sometimes struggle
when we begin to change.

RENATA
Right. Right.

She nods. This is a thing she has heard many times, in
many sessions. It has become belief now — the way doctrine
becomes belief.

McGEE
How are you sleeping?

RENATA
Not great. I keep having this dream
where I'm trying to speak and nothing
comes out. And I'm —

(small laugh)

I know, I know, you're going to
say that's a breakthrough.

McGEE
(gently)
I'm going to say that's information.

She smiles at him. The smile of the devoted.

What the audience sees: a patient who has grown and changed,
who trusts her therapist.

What we see: Renata is using McGee's language to describe
the erosion of every relationship she had before him. She
is not growing. She is being cleared.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S HOME STUDY - DAY (INTERVIEW)

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
I want to ask you about something
specific. There have been three
formal complaints filed with the —

McGEE
I know about the complaints.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
— with the Better Business Bureau,
since you're not licensed through —

McGEE
I'm not a licensed therapist.
I've never claimed to be.

(a careful precision)

I'm a life architect. That's the
designation I use. It's on every
piece of material I produce.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
And the people who come to you
believing they're receiving clinical
care?

McGEE
No one who does their homework
would believe that.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Most people don't do their homework
when they're in crisis.

McGEE
(a beat)
No. They don't.

He holds the journalist's gaze.

McGEE (CONT'D)
Which is why the relationship of
trust is so important. What I offer
is that trust. Completely. Every session.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
And if that trust is misplaced?

                                        GLITCH.

McGEE
Then I've done my job.

                                        BACK.

McGEE (CONT'D)
Then I encourage the person to
seek additional support. I always
have. It's in the framework.

He pauses.

McGEE (CONT'D)
The framework has a lot in it.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S OFFICE - DAY

SESSION: CAROL WHITMORE - MONTH ONE

CAROL WHITMORE (47) sits across from McGee with a tissue
in her hand that she isn't using. Her posture is the
posture of a woman who has held herself together for a
long time and is very tired of it.

She is warm-faced, trying to be. A sweater with a small
embroidered bird on it.

CAROL
My daughter thinks this is a mistake.
Coming to you.

McGEE
Tell me why she thinks that.

CAROL
She read something online. Some forum.
She said —

(a small laugh)

She said you're a cult.

McGEE
(smiling)
What do you think?

CAROL
I think I haven't slept through the
night in four years and I'm willing
to try anything.

McGEE
That's not a small thing to say.

CAROL
No. I guess it isn't.

He looks at her for a long moment.

McGEE
I want you to know something, Carol.
From our very first session. Whatever
we do here — however long it takes —
I am not going anywhere.

She looks at him.

McGEE (CONT'D)
I am not going to get tired of you.
I am not going to give up. I am not
going to decide you're too much.

Her eyes fill.

McGEE (CONT'D)
You are not too much.

Carol Whitmore begins to cry — not performing it,
not managing it. Just crying, in this warm light,
in front of this man who just said the thing no one
has said to her in a very long time.

He hands her the tissue box. She takes one.

What the audience sees: a moment of genuine human
connection. A woman beginning to feel safe.

What we will later understand: McGee has just made
a promise he has made to seventy-two other patients.
He keeps all of them. That is the system.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S HOME STUDY - DAY (INTERVIEW)

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Let me ask you something personal,
if that's alright.

McGEE
Everything I do is personal. Go ahead.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Have you ever been in therapy
yourself?

McGEE
(a small pause)
No.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Is that a choice?

McGEE
It's a conclusion. I reached it early
and I've revisited it often. The
conclusion hasn't changed.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Which is?

McGEE
That the only person I would trust
with my psychology is someone I would
have to acknowledge as my equal. And I
haven't found that person.

He says this without arrogance. As a fact.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
That's a lonely position.

A long pause. The longest of the interview.

McGEE
(quietly)
Yes.

For the first time, something real crosses his face. Not
the managed warmth, not the practiced gravity. Something
older and more private.

Then it's gone.

McGEE (CONT'D)
I find the work sustaining.

                                        GLITCH.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Dr. McGee — do you like what you do
to people?

McGEE
(a beat. Then, simply:)
Yes.

                                        BACK.

McGEE (CONT'D)
The work is everything. I can't
imagine doing anything else.

He lifts his coffee. It's gone cold. He sets it back down.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Last question.

McGEE
Of course.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
What would you tell someone who
was thinking about coming to you?

He thinks about this. Really thinks — the way he does when
he wants you to believe he's thinking, and perhaps he is.

McGEE
I'd tell them: come ready to be
uncomfortable. Come ready to hear
things about yourself that will
not feel good.

(beat)

And come ready to stay.

He looks directly at the camera — the first time — and
smiles.

McGEE (CONT'D)
Because the work takes time.

The smile holds.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S OFFICE - DAY

SESSION: CAROL WHITMORE - MONTH SEVEN

The same office. A different Carol.

Seven months have changed something that is hard to name.
She is not worse in any obvious way. She is smaller —
the way a thing becomes smaller when its center has been
removed and replaced with something else.

She is here for her second session this week. She comes
twice a week now, has since month four.

CAROL
Denise called again.

Her daughter.

McGEE
Mm.

CAROL
She wants me to come for Thanksgiving.

McGEE
How does that feel?

CAROL
Like — I want to. I want to see her.
But she said if I bring it up — if
I mention the work — she's going to
say things. And I can't — I'm not
in a place where I can absorb that
kind of energy right now.

McGEE
What kind of energy?

CAROL
Negative. Critical. She doesn't
understand where I am in the process.
The people closest to us often can't.

She says this the way McGee says it. The cadence is his.

McGEE
What do *you* think, Carol? Not
what the process says. What do you want?

CAROL
(a flicker — she tries)
I want my daughter not to look at
me like I'm — like I've lost something.

McGEE
And have you?

She looks at him. A long moment where the answer might
be real.

CAROL
(finally)
No. I've gained something.
I'm in the process. I'm —

The moment closes. She is back inside the language.

McGEE
(gently)
Good.

She nods. He has given her something. It does not feel
like a leash. It never does.

CUT TO:

INT. COMMUNITY CENTER - EVENING

The support group. Ten chairs. NADIA OSEI (34) sits
among them for the second time. She is still. Watching.

Carol is speaking.

CAROL
I've been having these thoughts.
At night, mostly. Really dark ones.

NADIA
What kind of thoughts?

The group looks at her. Nadia doesn't usually speak.

CAROL
Brian says they're part of the
process. The darkness is just the
old self resisting.

NADIA
What do they feel like? The thoughts.
Outside of what Brian says.

A beat. Carol tries to inhabit the distinction.

CAROL
Scary.

NADIA
You said scary first.

A flicker. Then the framework closes over it.

CAROL
I trust the process.

Nadia writes in her notebook. One word. Underlined twice.

*IDEATION.*

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S OFFICE - DAY

SESSION: CAROL WHITMORE - MONTH ELEVEN

The session that breaks something.

Carol enters differently. There's a quality in her
movement — not energy, exactly, but urgency, which
in Carol has always looked like a held breath.

She sits. She doesn't take off her coat.

CAROL
I called Denise.

McGEE
When?

CAROL
Last week. I — I didn't tell you.
I just called her.

McGEE
(a slight adjustment in his attention)
How did it go?

CAROL
She came over. We had dinner.
She cooked.

(a pause)

It was... it was good, Brian.

McGEE
Good how?

CAROL
Normal good. Like before. Like —
we were just sitting there eating
and she was telling me about her
job and I wasn't thinking about the
process or where I was in it.
I was just eating.

A silence. McGee looks at her.

McGEE
And that felt —?

CAROL
Like I hadn't felt in a while.

McGEE
(carefully)
Tell me something, Carol. In that
dinner, with Denise — did you talk
about the work you've been doing?

CAROL
She asked. I said I was — I said
I was doing well.

McGEE
You didn't tell her about the intensive?

CAROL
She would have — it would have
become a whole thing.

McGEE
So you protected the relationship
by concealing part of yourself.

CAROL
(something shifts)
I — what?

McGEE
I'm not criticizing. I'm
observing. The work we've done here
has taught you to protect your
process from external interference.
That's healthy. That's growth.

CAROL
But I wasn't — I wasn't protecting
the process. I was just having dinner.

McGEE
Were you though?

She stares at him.

McGEE (CONT'D)
Carol. Your first instinct — when
she asked — was to conceal. That
instinct exists for a reason. It
exists because this room is safe.
Out there —

A gesture toward the window.

McGEE (CONT'D)
— people are invested in your stasis.
Even the people who love you. Especially them.

CAROL
(very quietly)
That's not — I don't think that's
what happened.

McGEE
What do you think happened?

A long silence. She is trying to hold the dinner. The
easy feeling. Her daughter's laugh. All of it is being
slowly reclassified as threat.

CAROL
(her voice breaking)
I think I just wanted one night
that was normal.

McGEE
(gently — so gently)
Of course you did. That longing
is completely understandable.

(beat)

And it's also information. About
where you still are in the process.

She begins to cry. Not the soft tears of month one.
Something harder than that. Something from a place she
didn't know was there.

He hands her the tissue box.

He watches her cry with the focus of a man cataloguing data.

CUT TO:

INT. HOSPITAL CORRIDOR - NIGHT

Nadia stands in a hallway smelling of antiseptic and
recycled air. She holds her phone with both hands.

A NURSE passes. Nadia stops her.

NADIA
Carol Whitmore. She was admitted
this evening —

NURSE
Are you family?

NADIA
No. I'm a — I'm a friend.

NURSE
I'm sorry. I can only give information
to family.

The nurse moves on. Nadia doesn't.

She stands there a long time.

Then she opens her phone. Opens a voice memo. Presses record.

Holds it to her mouth.

NADIA
(recording)
Carol Whitmore. Admitted tonight.
Eleven months in the intensive.
She tried to tell him it was working.
He told her it was resistance.

She stops recording. Stares at the floor.

NADIA (CONT'D)
(quietly, not recording)
I should have moved faster.

She stands. Walks toward the exit.

Through the glass of a room she passes, we see Carol —
asleep, a monitor blinking, her daughter DENISE (28) sitting
beside her holding her hand.

Nadia doesn't stop. She can't help here now.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S HOME STUDY - DAY (INTERVIEW — FINAL SEGMENT)

The light has shifted. Afternoon now. The interview
has been going for hours.

The journalist looks tired. McGee does not. McGee never
looks tired in the presence of someone worth performing for.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Dr. McGee. I've been sitting here for
four hours. I've asked you about your
methods, your critics, your patients.
And you've been —

(a beat)

— extraordinary, actually. In the way
you handle questions.

McGEE
Thank you.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
I mean that as a professional
observation, not a compliment.

He absorbs this. Nods slightly.

JOURNALIST (O.S.) (CONT'D)
So let me try something different.
I'm going to ask you something
and I'd like you not to perform
an answer. Just —

McGEE
(amused, steady)
I'll do my best.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
What do you actually think of the
people who come to you?

A very long pause.

McGEE
That depends on the day.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Today.

McGEE
(carefully)
Today I think they are brave people
in tremendous pain, reaching for
something real in a world that
has failed them.

                                        GLITCH.

McGEE (CONT'D)
Today I think they are the most
useful people I have ever encountered.

                                        BACK.

McGEE (CONT'D)
And I feel — genuinely — the weight
of that trust. Every day.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Do you ever —

                                        GLITCH.

JOURNALIST (O.S.) (CONT'D)
Do you enjoy it? The control?

McGEE
(a pause. Then:)
Yes.

The most honest thing he has said in four hours.

It could mean either answer.

                                        BACK.

The journalist clicks off her recorder.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
Thank you for your time.

McGEE
Thank you for the questions.

He stands. Extends his hand. She shakes it.

JOURNALIST (O.S.)
(almost to herself)
You're very good at this.

McGEE
(a small smile)
I know.

She leaves. The room holds him.

He goes to the window. Looks at nothing.

Then he takes out his phone. Dials.

McGEE (CONT'D)
(into phone)
Get me the current patient list.
Flag anyone who's had external contact
in the last thirty days.

He listens.

McGEE (CONT'D)
No. I mean anyone.

He hangs up.

Stands at the window. The afternoon light flattens.

He is still. Unreadable. Entirely himself.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S OFFICE - DAY (SESSION 6 — NADIA)

This is the last session.

Both of them know it.

The office is the same. The same warm light, the same
white noise machine, the same distance between the two
chairs arranged as if they face each other as equals.

They do not face each other as equals. They never have.
But today the imbalance has shifted and McGee can feel it
the way you feel weather.

NADIA
How are you?

She never asks this. He notices.

McGEE
Fine. You?

NADIA
Clear.

McGEE
Clear is interesting.

He begins the session. He cannot not begin the session —
it is what he does, like breathing.

McGEE (CONT'D)
Tell me about this week.

NADIA
I've been thinking about the
people who came before me.
In this chair.

McGEE
What about them?

NADIA
What happens to them.

A silence.

McGEE
What do you think happens to them?

NADIA
Some of them get better. On their
own, or somewhere else. Some of them
don't.

McGEE
That's true of any therapeutic relationship.

NADIA
It's truer here.

He is very still now.

McGEE
What makes you say that?

NADIA
Because you're very good. And
because the better you are, the
more damage you can do without
anyone being able to name it.

A long, charged silence.

McGEE
(carefully)
Are you talking about yourself?

NADIA
No.

She reaches into her bag.

Sets her phone on the table between them.

Sets a small recorder next to it. Old, analog, a reporter's
device.

And then a photograph — printed, not on a phone. Carol
Whitmore, in a hospital bed. Denise at her side.

McGee looks at the photograph for a long time.

McGEE
(quietly)
Where did you get this?

NADIA
Denise sent it to me.

Beat.

NADIA (CONT'D)
Carol's going to be okay. She's
out of the hospital. She's with
her daughter. She's not coming back here.

McGEE
(measured)
I'm glad she's alright. I want —

NADIA
I know what you want.

He stops.

NADIA (CONT'D)
I know what you want for all of them.
I know the intake metric you use to
prioritize isolated patients. I know
the six phases. I know what "intensive
care" actually means in your system.

She picks up the recorder.

NADIA (CONT'D)
I have Carol on this. Eleven months,
in her own words.

McGEE
(a different quality now — the warmth
is not gone, exactly, but it has become
a material he is wearing, not a state
he is in)
That recording isn't admissible in —

NADIA
I know. I'm not a lawyer. I'm not
going to court.

McGEE
Then what are you doing?

NADIA
I'm finishing a session.

She stands. Gets her coat.

McGEE
Nadia.

She pauses.

McGEE (CONT'D)
You understand that whatever you
think you've uncovered —

NADIA
I don't think. I know.

McGEE
— there is no version of this
where you win. You have one recording
of a grief-compromised patient.
I have forty-three million listeners
and a legal team that does nothing
else.

NADIA
(turning to face him)
I know.

She buttons her coat.

NADIA (CONT'D)
I also know you're going to spend
the next week deciding what to do
about me. And while you're doing that,
Denise is going to spend that week
talking to Carol. And Carol is going
to start remembering things in a
different order.

McGee says nothing.

NADIA (CONT'D)
And the journalist who interviewed
you this morning is going to receive
an email tonight. From me.

A beat.

NADIA (CONT'D)
With some questions she didn't know
to ask.

She picks up the recorder. The photograph. Leaves the
phone.

NADIA (CONT'D)
(at the door)
The recording is a copy.

She walks out.

The door closes.

CUT TO:

INT. McGEE'S OFFICE - CONTINUOUS

McGee sits in the chair.

The phone is on the table. Nadia left it intentionally —
a message.

He looks at it for a long time.

Then he looks at the chair she sat in. The chair Thomas
sat in. The chair Renata sat in. The chair Carol sat in,
twice a week, for eleven months.

His face does something rare.

Not fear. Not remorse. Something quieter and less useful
than either.

Recognition.

The brief, unfamiliar experience of being seen by someone
who did not flinch at what they saw — and did not look away.

It lasts five seconds.

Then it's gone.

He picks up his phone. Dials.

McGEE
Clear my week. And get me Richard.

(beat)

The lawyer.

He stands. Goes to the window.

The machine is already running.

CUT TO BLACK.

TITLE CARD:       FLIPPED

                                                      BLACK.

Then, one by one, in silence:

TITLE CARD:
*Dr. Brian McGee's podcast reached 44 million listeners
the week this interview was published.*

TITLE CARD:
*The journalist's follow-up piece ran six weeks later.
It was the most-read article in the magazine's history.*

TITLE CARD:
*McGee's legal team issued a cease and desist
within 48 hours.*

TITLE CARD:
*Carol Whitmore did not return to the practice.*

TITLE CARD:
*Renata still does.*

TITLE CARD:
*There is no licensing board for
"Integrative Reality Therapy."*

TITLE CARD:
*It is not a recognized clinical method.*

TITLE CARD:
*Dr. Brian McGee's waiting list
currently has a four-month lead time.*

FADE TO BLACK.

THE END
